Steward @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (vms-steward, @ARCH@) - EARLY TEST RELEASE
=========================================================================

Steward manages OpenVMS from a web browser. It opens on a dashboard of the
system (OS and hardware, uptime, CPU, memory, paging, disks, processes, queues,
cluster), and covers users and rights identifiers, roughly what AUTHORIZE does:

  - sign in with your OpenVMS username and password (SYS$ACM: expiry, DISUSER,
    login hours and break-in evasion all apply); an expired password can be
    changed at sign-in
  - switch your authorized privileges on and off for the session, as
    SET PROCESS/PRIVILEGE does
  - list, filter and read users (everything AUTHORIZE SHOW shows) and rights
    identifiers with their holders
  - create users (as AUTHORIZE ADD), copy them (as COPY) and delete them (as
    REMOVE: with their UIC identifier and holdings)
  - modify users (owner, flags, days and hours, privileges, quotas, ...), set
    passwords, grant and revoke identifiers, create, rename and delete identifiers
  - access history: who reached the system, when, from where and by which route
    (SSH, TELNET, DECnet, LAT, batch, Steward), from accounting, the audit
    journal, OpenSSH's logs and Steward's own log
  - logical names: the shareable tables, the names in each and what a name
    translates to, as SHOW LOGICAL shows them to you; define, change and
    deassign names (as DEFINE and DEASSIGN), with the privileges you switched
    on: SYSNAM for supervisor and executive mode, GRPNAM or GRPPRV for your
    group table
  - processes: the ones OpenVMS lets you see (your own; your group's with GROUP
    switched on; everyone's with WORLD), as SHOW SYSTEM lists them, and one as
    SHOW PROCESS/ALL shows it; stop one, as STOP/ID (never Steward's own or a
    system process)
  - queues: every batch and print queue as SHOW QUEUE lists them, a queue's jobs
    and one job as SHOW ENTRY/FULL, as the queue manager lets you read them

Every request runs as you: Steward takes on your OpenVMS identity (a persona) for
the request, so OpenVMS decides what you may see and change, exactly as at a
terminal. Steward has no permissions of its own. Source, design and security
records: https://github.com/issinoho/vms-steward

This is a test release. It has been exercised on one x86-64 system. Please try it
on a test system first, and report problems at the address above.

Requirements: OpenVMS x86-64 (V9.2-x), VSI TCP/IP Services, VSI SSL3 (OpenSSL 3.0),
and lighttpd for OpenVMS (vms-lighttpd, https://github.com/issinoho/vms-lighttpd),
which serves the pages and passes the API to Steward. Browsers must reach Steward
over HTTPS: either through a reverse proxy that ends TLS (e.g. nginx proxy
manager), or with TLS in lighttpd.

Installed files (STEWARD$ROOT, defined by STEWARD$STARTUP)
---------------------------------------------------------
  [BIN]STEWARD.EXE             the FastCGI server (run as a pool of processes)
  [COM]STEWARD$CONFIGURE.COM   one-time setup, as SYSTEM
  [COM]STEWARD$CONTROL.COM     START, STOP, RESTART, STATUS
  [COM]STEWARD_POOL.COM        the pool (used by STEWARD$CONTROL)
  [COM]LOGIN.COM               copied to the service account (see below)
  [WWW]                        the browser front end (static files)
  [CONF]                       reference copies: STEWARD.CONF (annotated lighttpd
                               rules), STEWARD_SETTINGS.TEMPLATE
  [DOC]README.VMS, LICENSE
  SYS$STARTUP:STEWARD$STARTUP.COM, STEWARD$SHUTDOWN.COM

Setting up
----------
Install and set up vms-lighttpd first. Then, as SYSTEM:

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONFIGURE

It asks for (or takes as parameters) the service account (default STEWARD) and
its UIC, a data directory (default SYS$SYSDEVICE:[STEWARD_DATA]), the port lighttpd
should serve Steward on (default 8443), lighttpd's account (see below), any
reverse proxies to trust, and whether to start the pool at boot (default NO; see
"Starting at boot"). It:

  - creates the service account: batch access only, RESTRICTED, DISCTLY, a
    random password nobody knows. Privileges IMPERSONATE, TMPMBX and NETMBX by
    default, and SYSPRV authorized but NOT default: Steward switches SYSPRV on
    only for the instant it creates a user's persona (OpenVMS needs it to read
    that user's UAF record), and checks after every request that it is off.
    An existing account keeps its UIC and password; its privileges are set as
    above, and its default directory becomes the data directory.
  - creates the data directory: LOGIN.COM (a RESTRICTED account must have one),
    [.RUN] (session store, audit log, settings, pool logs; private to the
    account), [.CONF] (the lighttpd rules)
  - makes a gateway secret (from OpenSSL's random generator), shared by the
    pool and lighttpd. lighttpd sends it with every API request and Steward
    refuses requests without it, so no other process on the system can talk to
    the pool directly and pretend to be a different client.
  - writes [.CONF]STEWARD.CONF, a lighttpd server block for the port, and the
    site settings, SYS$MANAGER:STEWARD$CONFIG.COM

Then add the line it prints to lighttpd's LIGHTTPD.CONF, after server.modules
(which must list mod_fastcgi and mod_setenv; vms-lighttpd's default does):

    include "/<device>/STEWARD_DATA/conf/steward.conf"

and:

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONTROL START
    $ @VMSLIGHTTPD$ROOT:[COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$CONTROL RESTART

Browse to https://<host>:<port>/ (or your proxy's address) and sign in.

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONTROL STATUS

shows the pool's processes and their privileges (they should be DETACH,TMPMBX,NETMBX:
SYSPRV off) and the end of the audit log.

lighttpd's account
------------------
Give STEWARD$CONFIGURE the account vms-lighttpd actually runs as (by default it
offers the one in SYS$MANAGER:VMSLIGHTTPD$CONFIG.COM, normally LIGHTTPD). It
makes that account the owner of [.CONF]STEWARD_GATEWAY.CONF, which holds the
gateway secret and is readable by its owner only. If lighttpd runs as any other
account, it cannot read that file, and lighttpd then refuses its whole
configuration and does not start: every site it serves goes down, not only
Steward. Check before restarting lighttpd:

    $ DIRECTORY/SECURITY <data>[.CONF]STEWARD_GATEWAY.CONF

The owner must be lighttpd's account (SHOW PROCESS/ID=<pid> of VMSLIGHTTPD shows
it). To fix it without running STEWARD$CONFIGURE again:

    $ SET SECURITY/OWNER=LIGHTTPD <data>[.CONF]STEWARD_GATEWAY.CONF;

Starting at boot
----------------
Two things are needed:

  1. Autostart: answer YES to "Start the pool at boot" in STEWARD$CONFIGURE.
     It is recorded in SYS$MANAGER:STEWARD$CONFIG.COM as
         $ steward_autostart == "YES"
     and you can change that line by hand later. With NO, STEWARD$STARTUP only
     defines STEWARD$ROOT at boot and the pool stays stopped.
  2. The calls, as SYSTEM, in SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:
         $ @SYS$STARTUP:STEWARD$STARTUP
     and in SYS$MANAGER:SYSHUTDWN.COM:
         $ @SYS$STARTUP:STEWARD$SHUTDOWN

Place the startup call where everything the pool needs is already up:
  - TCP/IP Services and SSL3 (the pool listens on 127.0.0.1 and uses OpenSSL);
  - the disk holding the data directory, mounted;
  - the queue manager and SYS$BATCH (a batch queue, a generic queue feeding
    one, or a logical name for one): STEWARD$STARTUP starts the pool by
    submitting a job to SYS$BATCH as the service account (SUBMIT/USER, which
    needs CMKRNL: SYSTEM has it at boot). If the queue is stopped, the pool
    starts when it is started.
Steward and lighttpd may start in either order, but vms-lighttpd must also start
at boot (vmslighttpd_autostart in its own settings). STEWARD$CONFIG.COM's
steward_node limits autostart to one cluster member.

The service account must keep batch access and must not be disabled: each pool
process is a login of that account. Its password is never needed.

After a reboot, check:

    $ @STEWARD$ROOT:[COM]STEWARD$CONTROL STATUS

Running STEWARD$CONFIGURE again
-------------------------------
It is safe to run again (as SYSTEM). It offers your earlier answers (from
SYS$MANAGER:STEWARD$CONFIG.COM), so pressing Return keeps them, autostart
included. The exception is lighttpd's account: it always offers the account
vms-lighttpd is set up to run as, and says so if the gateway file belonged to
another.
  - Kept: the account's UIC and password, the gateway secret, and the whole of
    [.RUN]STEWARD_SETTINGS.COM. So it does not ask about trusted proxies again:
    to change them, edit STEWARD_TRUSTED_PROXIES there and RESTART the pool.
  - Rewritten: the account's privileges and default directory, STEWARD.CONF,
    STEWARD_GATEWAY.CONF (owned by the lighttpd account you give) and
    SYS$MANAGER:STEWARD$CONFIG.COM (autostart).
Then restart the pool (STEWARD$CONTROL RESTART), and lighttpd if the port or its
account changed.

Behind a reverse proxy
----------------------
Point the proxy at lighttpd's Steward port, and give STEWARD$CONFIGURE the address
the proxy connects from. It goes in STEWARD_SETTINGS.COM as
STEWARD_TRUSTED_PROXIES; to change it after the first run, edit it there and
RESTART the pool. Steward then believes the proxy's X-Forwarded-For, -Proto,
-Host and -Prefix headers, so break-in evasion and the audit log name the real
client. Never list an address untrusted clients can connect from.

The audit log
-------------
<data>[.RUN]AUDIT.LOG records every sign-in, sign-out, privilege switch and change,
with the user, the client address and OpenVMS's answer. Passwords are never
recorded.

Notes for this test release
---------------------------
- x86-64 only for now; IA64 will follow.
- Dates are shown in the browser's time zone. OpenVMS stores UAF dates in local
  time and keeps only the current offset from UTC, so a date from the other side
  of a daylight-saving change shows an hour out, as in other OpenVMS tools.
- Steward shows what OpenVMS lets you see, no more and no less: any signed-in
  user can read the rights database, and can tell whether a username exists,
  as at a terminal.
- Logical names you define from Steward are as if defined at a terminal: they
  can change what any process on the system translates, Steward's own included.
  Keep Steward's service account in a UIC group of its own, so that no other
  user's group logical names apply to it.
- Listing a logical name table runs SHOW LOGICAL in a short-lived detached
  process that logs in as you: it appears in accounting, counts against your
  process limits while it runs, and runs your SYLOGIN and LOGIN.COM first.

Removing
--------
    $ PRODUCT REMOVE STEWARD

stops the pool and deassigns STEWARD$ROOT. The data directory, the account and the
site settings are kept; remove the include line from LIGHTTPD.CONF by hand.
